    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "80a0056b_4600c733",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/DrawWriter.h",
        "patchSetId": 30
      },
      "lineNbr": 361,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-04-28T19:12:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think this zero\u0027ing could be brittle. I think it\u0027s meant to zero out the final tail that we want for the reservation that triggered the new buffer, but if the DBM\u0027s buffer size has bumped up to a pretty large buffer, then there\u0027s likely to be a fair amount of extra data that subsequent reservations can use (and may have different strides in).  Also think the current logic is off because `countDiff` is based off of `fPendingCount`, but if the DBM has made a new buffer, `writer` will be pointing to that instead.\n\nYour idea to query the DBM if it has enough space seems simplest, and if there isn\u0027t space for the allocation then it can call the existing `pad()` function.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 359,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 361,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "3506a82ddd35d9fce353d2feb113bfdaa8053915",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "99cc24a0_ad800d37",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/DrawWriter.h",
        "patchSetId": 30
      },
      "lineNbr": 361,
      "author": {
        "id": 3948337
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-04-28T19:30:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Apologies for confusion, I am using a different technique now: \"always zero, sometimes pad.\" Basically whenever we make a reservation we always zero out the tail according to whatever the reserve count was. Whether or not that data gets overwritten depends on if pad() is called.\n\nSo for this call here, we are not trying to zero out the data in preparation for the flush, that was *already* zeroed during its `reserve` call. Instead, we are preemptively zeroing the new buffer, in case it also gets flushed prior to `newPipelineState()`. \n\n\nThe advantage of this padding/zero technique is that it constrains the padding/zeroing logic to `DrawWriter`. Querying the DBM would also work, and I can implement that way as well. (If you have concerns about redundant zeroing, because we are zeroing on every `reserve` call now. DBM querying would mean repeating the overflow buffer logic.)",
      "parentUuid": "80a0056b_4600c733",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 359,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 361,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "3506a82ddd35d9fce353d2feb113bfdaa8053915",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "04280a03_9b273b11",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/DrawWriter.h",
        "patchSetId": 30
      },
      "lineNbr": 361,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-04-28T20:14:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I am concerned that double writing everything will have a noticeable impact on performance. In particular, cases where we just reserve(1), append(1), ... will mean we end up touching every byte twice. Now maybe that will not make any noticeable change in performance if the memory controller is able to discard the redundant zero\u0027ing for bytes we end up setting attributes on.  This would be something that we could hopefully measure with `nanobench` to make sure we weren\u0027t regressing.\n\nOn the other hand, alignment math is just a few instructions so repeating it is probably negligible. It could still be limited to the appendVerts case.\n\nI agree that adding a query for space left is a not the cleanest API for DBM, but isn\u0027t too unreasonable either. An alternative would be that we let DBM handle all of the zero\u0027ing internally, but I think that would be trickier. It\u0027d make the end-of-a-buffer simpler, but the padding and calculations for when a pipeline changes would end pushing a lot of tracking from DrawWriter back into DBM.\n\nIn the long term, if we move DBM to a ScratchBuffer approach, it\u0027ll end up similarly to the check-first and then request logic above. In that eventual world, `Appender` would store a mapped ScratchBuffer. `append()` would advance the cursor within the scratchbuffer if there was room, or it would fail. Then Appender would request a new buffer. `reserve` would no-op if the current buffer had enough room, or would request a new buffer. This would simplify things because the Appender can perform intermediate logic between running out of room and returning the used-up buffer, and when it\u0027s checked out a buffer, it knows about the entire size of the buffer and can use all of it.  Right now the fReserveCount and vertex returning makes that tracking harder to follow, but with a ScratchBuffer it tracks the used+written position exactly.",
      "parentUuid": "99cc24a0_ad800d37",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 359,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 361,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "3506a82ddd35d9fce353d2feb113bfdaa8053915",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
